{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And he called for Moses and Aaron by night</b> — this teaches that he was going around the entrances of the city and crying out, \"Where does Moses stay? Where does Aaron stay?\" This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 12:31). And this was because Moses and Aaron were [still] in Egypt that night, to fulfill his words that he had said (Exodus 11:8): \"And all these your servants shall come down to me and bow down to me, saying.\" And when Pharaoh came to them, they sent messengers to the land of Goshen where the children of Israel were, to give them permission to leave, and they all assembled in Rameses, and it was a great day, and from there they journeyed with a high hand and Moses at their head, as it is said (Numbers 33:3): \"And they journeyed from Rameses in the first month, on the fifteenth day of the month; on the morrow of the Passover the children of Israel went out with a high hand in the sight of all Egypt.\" And the verse that says (Deuteronomy 16:1), \"HaShem your Elohim brought you out of Egypt by night\" — because from the time Pharaoh released them they were called \"those who go out.\"\nAnd thus they said in the Sifre (see 128): Now did they not go out only by day, as it is said (Numbers 33:3), \"on the morrow of the Passover the children of Israel went out\"? Rather, this teaches that they were redeemed at night. And in the Gemara in tractate Berakhot (9a): all agree that when they were redeemed, they were redeemed only at night, as it is said (Deuteronomy 16:1), \"HaShem your Elohim brought you out of Egypt by night,\" and when they went out, they went out only by day, as it is said, \"on the morrow of the Passover the children of Israel went out with a high hand in the sight of all Egypt.\" And there are those who say that they departed from Egypt at night, and from the land of Egypt, which is Rameses, by day, for many of them dwelt in the city of Egypt, and they went out at night and assembled with their brothers in Rameses. But this is not correct, for the verse says (Exodus 12:22), \"And none of you shall go out from the door of his house until morning\" — they were forbidden to go out from the houses at all at night. And thus they said in the Mekhilta (Pisḥa 13): \"And he called for Moses and Aaron by night and said, 'Rise up, go out' — Moses said to him: Thus we were commanded, 'And none of you shall go out from the door of his house until morning.' Are we thieves, that we should go out at night? We shall not go out except with a high hand in the sight of all Egypt.\" And Onkelos explained that the wonders that were done for them at night are called \"brought you out,\" for on account of them they went out." "And the sense of <b>Rise up, go out from among my people</b> is the king's decree that they should leave immediately, for I will not give you permission to remain among my people at all, for they are being killed on your account." "<b>Both you</b> — the elders; <b>and also the children of Israel</b> — all of them shall go out, and you shall not linger here in any manner. And this is to fulfill \"he will surely thrust you out from here\" (Exodus 11:1)." "And he said, <b>Go, serve YHWH, as you have said</b> — in appeasement, that they should want to go out and listen to him, for you desire to go to the wilderness to serve YHWH."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The abbreviation ראה קכח likely refers to Sifre section 128; resolved by context.", 2 "Ramban reads המכים as \"the elders\"; this may be a scribal variant for הזקנים, or understood metonymically."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:07:08.091463520Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}